description:
Raised by decision 0011, Questions for the project owner #1. Three things currently in the repo cannot all hold:

- the volunteer mockup asks for 'no user accounts / phone app needed' and 'can be accessed from anywhere';
- volunteers are a pick-list with no password (decision 0008 point 5);
- the backend's DEFAULT_PERMISSION_CLASSES is IsAuthenticated.

Someone has to decide whether the batch endpoint is reachable by anyone who can reach the site, whether the site sits behind something at the network or proxy layer, or whether a single shared credential is issued. The QR flow design works unchanged under any of these - the question is who NYC Mesh is willing to let write to its ledger, which is not a decision for an implementer.

Does NOT block implementation: the endpoints are built with the project default and relaxing it is one line. It DOES need answering before volunteers use the system.

loadDetector() in frontend/src/scan/decoder.ts memoises the promise rather than its result:

    detector ??= (async () => { await import("barcode-detector/pure"); ... })();

A single failed dynamic import -- a flaky connection in a basement, or a stale index.html after a deploy pointing at a chunk that is no longer served -- leaves `detector` holding a permanently rejected promise. Every later call answers with the same rejection: after Stop and Start, after a lens switch, after the volunteer closes and reopens the scanner. Nothing short of reloading the page clears it, because forgetDetector() exists only for tests and its own comment says so.

What the volunteer sees, now that CameraScanner releases the stream when the open fails (inventory-tng-w1e): the camera opens, the warning appears, and every retry fails identically for the life of the tab.

APPROACH

Clear the memo when the load rejects, so the next open retries and a successful load is still kept:

    detector ??= load().catch((error: unknown) => {
      detector = null;
      throw error;
    });

Worth checking at the same time whether the failure reaches the volunteer as something better than refusal()'s fallback -- 'the camera could not be opened' is not what happened, and 'the scanner could not be downloaded; try again' names the way out.

Raised by the xhigh review of inventory-tng-w1e, which found it while reviewing the code that now depends on the retry working.
